  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107543,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:53:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107544,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients may show manifestations of a pelvic mass, as well as estrogenic effects in 80% of cases, including precocious pseudopuberty, menstrual disturbances, or virilization {6465418}. JGCTs are predominantly unilateral (&gt;&nbsp;95% of cases) and rarely spread beyond the ovary. However, haemoperitoneum secondary to tumour rupture is an occasional presentation. Rare patients have <em>DICER1</em> syndrome, Maffucci syndrome, or Ollier disease {21501861; 1553918; 6365306}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:53:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107545,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>JGCTs usually occur within the first three decades of life (mean age: 13&nbsp;years), and 50% occur in prepubertal patients. They represent approximately 5% of all ovarian granulosa cell tumours {6465418}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:53:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107546,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:53:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107547,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "<p>Activating alterations in <em>AKT1</em> and <em>GNAS</em> have been detected in 60% and 30% of tumours, respectively {26137586; 16507630}. Somatic mosaic <em>IDH1</em> and <em>IDH2</em> mutations have been reported in tumours associated with Ollier disease or Maffucci syndrome {31409083}. Rare reports describe an association with tuberous sclerosis and germline <em>TP53</em> and <em>PTEN</em> mutations {16516278; 18669439}. Somatic <em>DICER1</em> mutations are rare {22187960; 26033501; 29275930}. FISH analysis may identify monosomy&nbsp;22 or trisomy&nbsp;12 {12218213}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-17T12:18:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107548,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The mean tumour size is 120&nbsp;mm (range: 30&ndash;320&nbsp;mm). JGCTs are typically solid or solid and cystic, and rarely predominantly cystic, with a tan to yellow to grey cut surface, commonly with areas of necrosis and haemorrhage (especially if ruptured) {6465418}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:53:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107549,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours show a nodular or diffuse architecture and scattered interspersed follicles of varying size with irregular contours, frequently containing basophilic secretions lined by cell layers of variable thickness. The stroma is typically inconspicuous but sometimes prominently sclerotic or myxoid. Tumour cells have round, vesicular, non-grooved nuclei, and (usually) abundant pale or eosinophilic cytoplasm. There is minimal to marked nuclear atypia with variable mitotic activity {6465418}. Variably luteinized and sometimes spindled theca cells may be seen. Occasional pseudopapillae may develop due to cystic degeneration {18301054}. Tumours usually express SF1, inhibin, calretinin, WT1, CD99, and CD56; some express EMA and FOXL2 (without associated mutation).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:53:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107550,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Prognosis and prediction</em>, below.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:53:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
